Z||000005||LarryB||08-24-2001||07:49 PM||||The University of Rochester has a trauma/burn unit, so they would be a suitable place to get transferred to.<P>A trauma unit has a bunch of specialized equipment on hand with a staff properly trained in how to use it 24x7, things like CT scanners, xray machines in the emergency room, and a helicopter pad so that mediflights can come in, supplies of exotic drugs, local labs, etc.<P>Burn victims have specific needs in the way their wounds are treated and the treatments they get, skin harvesting and grafting, checking for infection, cleaning and debriding of the wounds, etc. One of the pieces of equipment I know of are large tanks of water they submurge burn victims in to help change their dressings and clean them up.  Probably the biggest difference though is that the people are specifically trained and experienced in how to treat people with bad burns.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by starfighter007@msn.com:<BR><B>That is really sad. How did this person burn?I wonder what is different between burn unit and non burn unit in a hospital? It must be more than medicine I guess.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
